  "note": "Re-judged 2026-11-03 by an independent auditor who authored none of 816ce1ce4c, 2cbd255ba4, 2e5c2d012a, d22b811ce4 or 1a2ccaeef8 (phase 5 of spec-filter-wire-0-umbrella, the per-peer text filter chain deleted). RFC 2545 Section 3 read: \"The value of the Length of Next Hop Network Address field on a MP_REACH_NLRI attribute shall be set to 16, when only a global address is present, or 32 if a link-local address is also included in the Next Hop field.\" Moved units TestFamilyNextHopForwardAdmission and TestRFC2545EffectivePairPolicyAndMixedSibling changed only the filter plumbing: The test filter is now an answerTransport behind filterTransportSeam, compiled by answerPolicy into a policychain library chain, so the verdict travels the real filter-update path (route.Input, rpc.FilterUpdateOutput decoded by the library chain) instead of the deleted policyFilterSeam that handed a PolicyResponse straight to the text chain; that is the production path every plugin filter now takes. The wrong-width fields (native IPv4, VPN-shaped 24 and 48 octets, empty, eight octets) still arrive as the export answer and are still withdrawn, never sent at their width; the superseded-* and raw-*-repaired positives still require the destination configured next hop to write the exact legal 16- or 32-octet field over the raw base, with the whole MP_REACH value, length octet included, compared. Assertions byte-identical. The interop carrier checkRFC2545NextHops is not in this verdict scope change; its revert record is reported separately by ./le rfc check (check_rfc.go, 29f15481fa) and is not phase 5 work. Ran the tagged units under ./le job run at HEAD (-tags ze_bgp, -v): PASS (scratch audit-p5-units-v.log). Verdict unchanged: enforced.",
